WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.340
(machine roaring)

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:05.760
- [Host] In November, the defense
startup Anduril Industries

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.450
released its latest autonomous
weapons systems, Roadrunner

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:10.533
and Roadrunner-M.

00:00:11.707 --> 00:00:13.890
(bombs exploding)

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:15.770
The Roadrunners join Anduril's suite

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:17.940
of AI-powered unmanned products

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:19.642
that are shaking up the defense industry.

00:00:19.642 --> 00:00:22.680
(machine roaring)

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.803
Since it was launched in 2017,

00:00:24.803 --> 00:00:28.410
Anduril has found success as a
defense department contractor

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:30.000
selling autonomous vehicles

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.041
and the software that powers them.

00:00:32.041 --> 00:00:34.879
Today, the startup's
technology is being used across

00:00:34.879 --> 00:00:37.470
multiple branches of the US Military,

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.980
and in both the Ukraine-Russia
and Israel-Gaza conflicts.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:44.193
The company was recently
valued at $8.48 billion.

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:48.360
Anduril was created to help
build a new type of military

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.790
to meet the demands of
a changing battlefield.

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:53.220
- We are in a position
where over the last decades,

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:54.900
we have built the wrong things.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.060
- We need a different kind of US military

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:58.920
and a different approach
to military power.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.290
- Anduril I think, is a healthy revolution

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:03.172
for the defense industry.

00:01:03.172 --> 00:01:05.190
(soft music)

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:06.450
- So this is Fury.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:08.700
It's an unmanned combat
aircraft that is able

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:11.820
to work in collaboration
with manned fighters,

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:14.850
with other manned aircraft
to hugely push out the range

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:16.620
that we can engage
targets at without putting

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:17.970
people at risk.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:19.980
That's kind of one of the
coolest things about autonomous

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.710
systems, not necessarily the
fact that they can think faster

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:24.000
or do better than a person,

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.280
but they can also just
throw away their lives.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.170
- [Host] Anduril's founder Palmer Luckey

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.470
is among the more polarizing
figures in the tech industry.

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:32.580
- My name is Palmer Luckey

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.260
and I'm a virtual reality enthusiast,

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:35.610
and the designer of the Rift.

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:38.250
- [Host] Luckey first found
success when his virtual reality

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.710
company, Oculus, was acquired by Facebook

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:42.690
for more than $2 billion.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:45.636
But in 2017, Luckey was
ousted from Facebook

00:01:45.636 --> 00:01:47.769
after a tumultuous tenure.

00:01:47.769 --> 00:01:49.320
(soft music)

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:50.760
- Let me just show you very quickly

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:51.840
what our software looks like.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.700
This is an example of what we think

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.380
it's a pretty intuitive interface.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:56.850
- [Host] The foundation of Anduril

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:58.950
is Lattice a software platform

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.200
that serves as a command center of sorts,

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.750
allowing a single human
operator to control

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:07.530
and coordinate multiple autonomous
robots, be they a drone,

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.420
a sub, or a mobile sentry.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:12.360
- What you're gonna see at the
bottom is essentially all the

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.250
different assets that
are a part of the team,

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:15.930
and then you're gonna
see a graphical display

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:17.760
that looks very similar
to a lot of the software

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.100
that we might use in everyday life,

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:21.720
where there's a map in the background.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:24.630
You're gonna see multiple
vehicles on that software,

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:25.800
and each of them, you kind of know

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:26.970
what role they're playing.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:30.480
Engineers are designing our
software, much like the people

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.160
who design the kinds of software

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:33.960
that goes on our cell
phones or on an iPad.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:36.537
It's meant to be very, very user friendly,

00:02:36.537 --> 00:02:39.618
hardware focused defense companies.

00:02:39.618 --> 00:02:41.730
That hasn't been the approached.

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.850
- The US military likes to buy things.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:48.660
The whole contract mechanism
is set up to buy things.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:52.140
And just now, I think
generationally, culturally,

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:53.820
they're starting to catch up to the idea

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:56.850
that a software package
can be just as powerful

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:58.320
as the thing that it comes in.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.180
- [Host] But Anduril's
Tech isn't the only thing

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:01.260
that sets it apart.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:03.513
The startup is also
pursuing a business model

00:03:03.513 --> 00:03:06.510
that borrows a lot of
inspiration from the tech world.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:09.270
- The way that the traditional defense,

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:12.030
really large defense companies
are set up when they take

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:16.140
risk, the market actually frowns
upon that risk being taken.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:18.420
We are designed completely
differently as a company.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.470
We are designed like a high-tech company,

00:03:22.013 --> 00:03:23.250
the set of investors that we have,

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:25.985
and ultimately, hopefully,
when we're a public company,

00:03:25.985 --> 00:03:29.760
it is expected that we take risk on.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.202
- [Host] Anduril says
it's taking bigger risks

00:03:32.202 --> 00:03:33.270
because it's betting

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:35.880
that the way we fight
wars is changing rapidly.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:38.130
- What we're dealing with right
now is a disruption created

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:41.400
by technology created by the
threats that we're facing.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:44.190
- [Host] Anduril's Chief
Strategy Officer Christian Brose,

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:45.480
likens this alternative

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:48.390
to the Moneyball system
described in Michael Lewis's

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:49.680
book of the same name.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:51.930
- You guys are talking
the same old nonsense.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:53.790
We got to think differently.

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:55.830
- [Host] In "Moneyball,"
the Oakland A's attempt

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:57.627
to put together a
baseball club on a budget

00:03:57.627 --> 00:04:00.142
by employing computer-generated analysis

00:04:00.142 --> 00:04:02.220
to draft its players.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:03.270
- Similar to baseball.

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:06.090
You have to come up with a
strategy that is low-cost,

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:09.771
that's going to allow you
to build an affordable,

00:04:09.771 --> 00:04:12.750
sort of scalable approach
that's going to be successful.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:14.250
For military terms,

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:18.270
it's not relying upon
traditional military systems,

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:20.100
that come in very small quantities,

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:22.920
that are rather large, very
expensive, very exquisite,

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:25.680
hard to produce, hard
to replace, very manned,

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:29.190
going the opposite direction,
and relying on larger

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:31.620
and larger quantities
of smaller, lower-cost,

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:33.414
more autonomous military systems

00:04:33.414 --> 00:04:35.460
that you can produce very quickly,

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:37.230
you can replace very quickly.

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:40.650
- Anduril I think, recognized
that there was a gap in

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:42.300
what some of the big primes were doing

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.310
and sought to fill that gap.

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:46.980
Thinking differently about
defense is something that's hard

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:49.380
to do if you've been very
successful working in your

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:51.570
particular way of thinking about defense.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:53.340
That's part of why
Anduril is so interesting

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:55.080
as an alternative mechanism.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:56.790
- [Host] Since the end of the Cold War,

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:58.890
America's defense industry has undergone

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:00.450
considerable consolidation.

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:01.650
In the 1990s,

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:04.500
the US relied on 51 primary contractors.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:06.780
Today, that number is just five.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:10.620
Lockheed Martin, Boeing, RTX
Corporation, Northrop Grumman,

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:12.720
and General Dynamics.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:15.510
According to a 2022 DOD report,

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:17.970
fewer contractors has stifled innovation

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:20.220
and undermined price competition.

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:23.106
- We live in a world where 80%
of weapons procurement goes

00:05:23.106 --> 00:05:24.990
to just five companies.

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:28.080
- [Host] One of the biggest
issues is cost-plus contracts.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.660
- Meaning they're paid for
time, materials, workforce,

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:32.550
and then a fixed percentage
of profit on top.

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:35.910
Because that profit is a plus, a lot

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:38.340
of vendors are incentivized
for the cost portion

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:40.080
to be as large as possible as well.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:41.970
They can often make more
money when something

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:43.440
takes longer than it was supposed to.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:45.420
They can often make more money
when they sell a solution

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:47.141
that's more expensive
rather than a solution

00:05:47.141 --> 00:05:49.410
that's less expensive.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:51.750
And this wouldn't be a
huge problem if there

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:53.130
was real competition.

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:55.688
- [Host] Anduril is carving
out an alternative pathway

00:05:55.688 --> 00:05:59.010
to secure DOD contracts by
buying up smaller startups

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:01.620
and small to medium sized businesses.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:03.150
- So when Anduril looks
at buying companies,

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:04.564
we typically want to buy companies

00:06:04.564 --> 00:06:07.170
that are quite far
along building something

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:10.680
that is differentiated, that
is far ahead of what we have,

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:12.660
and that would be difficult
for us to catch up with.

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:14.850
And so what it really comes
down to is do they have

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:17.760
something that will become
much more valuable when paired

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:20.130
with our core product,
which is Lattice AI,

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:21.990
can autonomy make this thing

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:24.810
that they're making into a
much more powerful product?

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:28.380
And that's been the basis of
all of our acquisitions so far.

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:31.500
- [Host] Today, Defense
contractors produce small numbers

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:33.990
of large, expensive, heavily manned

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:36.030
hard to produce weapons systems.

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:38.040
The so-called Moneyball Military

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:39.990
that companies like
Anduril are working toward

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:40.823
will be made up

00:06:40.823 --> 00:06:44.700
of large numbers of smaller,
lower cost autonomous systems.

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:46.740
- I don't think that
Anduril will ever be able

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:51.030
to completely replace one
of the prime contractors.

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:55.200
What they do is very
specific and very well honed.

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:57.900
We, unfortunately, are
in a very uncertain world

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:00.750
where great power
competition is a potential.

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:02.250
It's happening right now,

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:04.320
and that great power
conflict is a potential

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:05.940
that we all have to deal with.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:07.680
And in that kind of world,

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:09.690
the prime contractors
are gonna be necessary

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:11.820
to build those huge defense systems.

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:13.980
- The large primes are struggling

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:15.180
to do things the way Anduril does

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:16.740
because they're publicly traded companies

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:19.170
with an existing investor
class that invested in them

00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:20.840
to be a certain type of company.

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:22.740
At the same time, I
hope they do get there.

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:24.570
I think that maybe they're not gonna get

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:26.160
quite to where Anduril is.

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:28.050
But I think that we could get to a future

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:30.570
where defense companies are
spending more on developing

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:32.880
their own products than the government is.

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:34.407
I think that's a positive world.

00:07:34.407 --> 00:07:37.074
(upbeat music)

